 "superseded_candidates": "2e022f5 (4822fffe) and 1d63b77 (a41e7b71): their runs are kept under evidence/fair-handoff/superseded-*. They exposed 4 defects in my own new code, all fixed before the release: (1) time refused before admission counted against an admitted writer; (2) ordinary work started after due work had spent the hold budget (E_SEAT_BINDING); (3) a confirmed cancel was worded as failed when only the settlement notice failed; (4) a lost queue counter could reuse a live entry's name. (4) was found by mutation testing.",
 "design": {
  "D2": "Numbered FIFO admission queue <db>.queue/ beside the flock. Only the oldest live entry may try the flock; the entry is dropped when the lock is taken, so a re-locking holder queues at the back. Caps are enforced numerically: run 1, due 3 (timer-callback, check, timeout-ack), ordinary 2. A full class is refused at once with E_LEDGER_SATURATED. An admitted writer waits at most AdmittedWait = 6 x 11 s = 66 s from its admission. Entries that are dead, pid-reused (start time differs), stale (not refreshed for 3 s) or malformed (moved to rejected/) are removed by whoever sees them. The queue mutex is a short flock; no daemon. Arrival order only, no class priority. When due work spends an ordinary writer's hold budget, the writer re-queues (up to 3 times). The ledger lock is still taken before a fresh load, and the 10 s hold budget is unchanged.",
  "D1": "The SATURATED stamp is written only on wake rc 0 or rc 3 (transport accepted, never an ack). Every attempt goes to .saturation-attempts. rc 1, a runner error or a budget refusal is retried by the next holder. A stamp write error is reported and never claims suppression.",
  "D3": "Each send has an incident key and a durable record written before the wake. Core wake: wake|reason|action. /cancel: stop|action. No-ack: noack|qid|role|timeout id|settled at. Ack expiry: ackexpiry|qid|id|deadline. Settlement failure: settlefail|qid|action. Other sends: exact text. After an unknown outcome: ONE labelled 'REPEAT (notice:...)' per incident across all restarts, then 'unresolved' (E_DELIVERY_UNRESOLVED), which is visible and never marked delivered or acked. Its due marker is kept aside as .unresolved-*.json, and the director is told that the settlement notice was not confirmed (the cancel wording stays correct). A /cancel is never repeated after an unknown outcome. Definite refusals: the first retry is immediate, then backoff 1, 2, 4... s, at most 5 sends. Budget refusals are 'not-started' and use no attempt."
 },
 "evidence": {
  "unit": "go test 0 FAIL (go-test.txt on 5da2b36, go-test-0a38a8a.txt). 11 new tests in internal/loop/handoff_p12_test.go: hand-off, cap refusal, dead/reused/stale/malformed entries, no leftover entry, lost counter, SATURATED rc1/error/budget/rc0/rc3 plus retry, repeat once across 2 crashes, no repeat of a cancel, bounded backoff, unresolved marker closed as owned failure with correct wording.",
  "mutation": "137 of 137 on 0a38a8a (mutate-go-0a38a8a.txt, rc 0); 136/137 on 5da2b36 (the lost-counter mutant had no test; a test was added). 14 new mutants. One old mutant was removed as now EQUIVALENT: 'expired ack escalated on every pass' (the ack-expiry incident key also stops the repeat); the reason is in mutate_go.py.",
  "conformance": "125/125, 1751 steps, rc 0, on the exact release binary 842e44d6 (conformance-release-binary/)",
  "parity": "316/321 + 5 adjudicated (host-parity.txt)",
  "regressions_on_842e44d6": "regress/: CLI timeout idle and refused with ack rules; bounded-deadline busy-callback race; writer race (changed input); stopped-mode no-ack escalation (duty 60 s, director 120 s, no incident); prompt SIGTERM stop 0.007 s. Calendar unit templates are unchanged byte for byte (examples/ identical to 487cb92) and were not re-run.",
  "real_process": {
   "harness": "fairhandoff.sh + analyse.py + run-all.sh (exact invocations, env), all committed before each batch ran: pre-registration commits 4742fed8, 2f79f757, 7cb3bc8f, 6a7269ad, 8e498c4a",
   "runs_on_release": "new-supported, new-overload, new-crash2, new-crash3 (842e44d6); old-overload and old-crash2 are the same workloads on b70665f5 (the D1/D2/D3 reproductions)",
   "results": "see plans/README.md table and each */analysis.txt. 14 accepted due markers per run, all settled in every run, max 67.3 s after the deadline (old 82.6 s). 8 of 8 yields handed the lock to a waiting dispatch (old: 0 of 3). Longest wait from admission to the lock: 46.2 s (dispatch), 44.5 s (run), under the 66 s bound. Run heartbeat age after the stall under overload: 52.0 s (old 135.5 s). Overload arrivals: 9 accepted, 14 refused at once with E_LEDGER_SATURATED (old: 14 E_LEDGER_BUSY after 60 s and 1 E_SEAT_BINDING budget failure). Local non-subprocess time in a hold: max 0.145 s over 305 holds (old 0.117 s). Run heartbeat age after the stall in crash3 (kills and a restart): 54.2 s. Max settlement in crash3: 59.0 s.",
   "transport_injection": "rc 1 refusals, rc 3 queued, hangs of 15 s (budget-capped, so the outcome is ambiguous but the effect lands), budget refusals (not-started records), the first duty notice refused (SATURATED rc 1 not stamped, then accepted as rc 3 or rc 0)",
   "crashes": "crash2: SIGKILL of the holder during the original deadline-expired:B01 notice, then SIGKILL of the next holder during its labelled REPEAT. Result: 2 effects (original + 1 labelled repeat), the third attempt refused -> unresolved, marker .unresolved-*, director told. crash3: BEFORE (holder and wake killed before the effect) -> 1 effect (the labelled repeat); DURING -> 2; AFTER (effect logged, receipt pending) -> 2. Every run has 14 cancels for 14 deadlines, no extra dispatch and one ledger settlement per package. A queued waiter was SIGKILLed and reaped; a SIGSTOPped waiter was reaped as stale, lost its place and re-queued; run was restarted with a new pid.",
   "admitted_work": "every ordinary arrival ended rc 0, E_LEDGER_SATURATED (refused at admission, visible to the caller) or rc 137 (killed by the harness). No admitted writer timed out in the release runs."
  }
 },
